#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Opens this repo's own tracker on the board and walks through one ticket that moved: what git
# recorded, what the board's loader now reads for it, and what the opened row says.
set -euo pipefail

here=$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)
root=$(cd "$here/../../../.." && pwd)
out=$here/out
ticket=agent/tickets/debrief-ticket.md

branch=$(git -C "$root" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)
[[ $branch != HEAD ]] || { echo "demo: $root is on no branch, and the board is rendered from a clone of one" >&2; exit 1; }

# The board renders the tracker of its repo's main checkout, and this ticket lives on a feature
# branch, whose main checkout is a worktree or a bare repo. A clone of the branch is a main checkout
# that holds it. Its ticket branches come back under refs/heads, which is where the board reads a
# ticket's questions from.
rm -rf "$out"
mkdir -p "$out"
git clone --quiet --shared --branch "$branch" \
    "$(git -C "$root" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)" "$out/repo"
git -C "$out/repo" for-each-ref --format='%(refname:lstrip=3)' 'refs/remotes/origin/ticket/**' |
    while read -r ref; do
        # a branch of that name is already here when it is the one the clone checked out
        git -C "$out/repo" branch --quiet "$ref" "origin/$ref" 2>/dev/null ||
            git -C "$out/repo" rev-parse --verify --quiet "$ref" >/dev/null
    done

recorded=$(git -C "$out/repo" log --all --follow --diff-filter=R --name-status -M --format='%as %s' -- "$ticket")
[[ -n $recorded ]] || { echo "demo: $branch records no move of $ticket, so there is nothing here to show" >&2; exit 1; }
"$here/sessions.py" "$out/repo" "$ticket" >"$out/sessions.txt"
named=$(grep -cv "no transcript here" "$out/sessions.txt" || true)

cat <<TEXT
This ticket moved on 2026-09-23: it was filed inside the feature board-orients as its slice 15, and
left the feature to stand on its own. What git wrote down for that commit, which is the whole of
what the board has to go on:

$(sed 's/^/    /' <<<"$recorded")

The old path on the left, the new one on the right. Until this slice the board keyed a session by
the path its commit changed, so the ticket on the right kept none of the sessions that worked on it
under the path on the left, and read as work nobody had touched. Now the move carries them over,
and this is what the board's loader reads for $ticket:

$(sed 's/^/    /' "$out/sessions.txt")

The first filed the ticket and the second moved it. The board lists the ones this machine holds a
transcript of, with the title the transcript gives them, because those are the ones it can hand you
a command that resumes: a session that ran on another host is left out.
TEXT

board_flags=(--no-watch --out "$out/board.html")
[[ -n ${DISPLAY:-}${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-} ]] || board_flags+=(--no-open)
"$root/mx/bin/board" "$out/repo/agent/tickets" "${board_flags[@]}"

cat <<TEXT

Open the page above and find "The debrief is a ticket". It sits in blocked while its blocker,
retro-semi-automated, is open, and in the frontier once that is ruled away. Click it.
TEXT
if [[ $named -gt 0 ]]; then
cat <<TEXT
Its *sessions on this machine* block lists the sessions named above, each with the days it committed
on the ticket and a button that copies the command resuming it. Until this slice the block held the
session that moved the ticket and no other, because every other one committed under the old path.
TEXT
else
cat <<TEXT
It has no *sessions on this machine* block. Both sessions above ran on another machine, so there is
nothing here to resume, and the board lists nothing rather than a command that would fail. On the
machine they ran on, the block is there with both.
TEXT
fi
cat <<TEXT

figure.html beside this script is that block before and after, side by side, on a demo tracker whose
sessions every machine can name.
TEXT
